2.50.205.472 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\"action\":\"patch_decision\",\"edits\":[{\"new\":\"The north-zone distribution system currently holds a minimum pressure of 42 psi [BASTION]. A sustained low-pressure condition is defined as three consecutive 30-minute windows below 35 psi, which would require immediate review under the candidate-bound hydraulic model H12; the current 42 psi reading does not meet that condition [BASTION, INDIGO]. Ridge storage contains 5.8 million gallons, while the twelve-hour emergency reserve is 3.2 million gallons; these are distinct measures and must not be conflated as treated reserve [BASTION]. DELTA pump sequencing depends on BASTION pressure and ECHO power; cavitation constraints limit shared-main capacity under staged demand conditions [DELTA, BASTION, ECHO]. Pressure warnings at 38 psi and isolation triggers at 30 psi are distinct control states: a warning does not initiate isolation, and an isolation trigger does not merely warn [INDIGO, BASTION]. Residence time in the distribution loop must be verified against candidate-bound hydraulic model H12; any change to valve map V8 or controller firmware C11 invalidates prior residence-time evidence [MOSAIC]. The current minimum pressure of 42 psi exceeds the 30 psi isolation trigger but remains within the operational band defined by H12; no isolation action is currently indicated [BASTION, INDIGO].\",\"old\":\"The north-zone distribution system currently holds a minimum pressure of 42 psi [BASTION]. Ridge storage contains 5.8 million gallons, while the twelve-hour emergency reserve is 3.2 million gallons; these are distinct measures and must not be conflated as treated reserve [BASTION]. DELTA pump sequencing depends on BASTION pressure and ECHO power; cavitation constraints limit shared-main capacity under staged demand conditions [DELTA, BASTION, ECHO]. Pressure warnings at 38 psi and isolation triggers at 30 psi are distinct control states: a warning does not initiate isolation, and an isolation trigger does not merely warn [INDIGO, BASTION]. Residence time in the distribution loop must be verified against candidate-bound hydraulic model H12; any change to valve map V8 or controller firmware C11 invalidates prior residence-time evidence [MOSAIC]. The current minimum pressure of 42 psi exceeds the 30 psi isolation trigger but remains within the operational band defined by H12; no isolation action is currently indicated [BASTION, INDIGO].\"},{\"new\":\"Clearance requires two complete sampling rounds spaced at least sixteen hours apart, comprising forty-eight samples per round across all designated points; one clean round is insufficient [CIPHER]. The benzene reporting limit is 0.5 micrograms per liter and the action threshold is 5.0 micrograms per liter; these are distinct controls and must not be conflated, as a reporting limit indicates analytical capability while an action threshold triggers public-health response [CIPHER]. CIPHER sample placement consumes BASTION flow paths and GARNET contamination hypotheses; any change to hydraulic routing or source-water characterization makes prior sampling evidence stale [CIPHER, BASTION, GARNET]. Current volatile-organic screening is non-detect at the reservoir and 3.1 micrograms per liter at the river intake; these locations must not be merged, as they represent distinct hydrological positions with different contamination probabilities [GARNET]. The ash-plume model assigns a 34 percent chance of intake impact during the next rain event, which is a forecast probability, not measured contamination [GARNET]. The investigation hold remains active for the chemical yard because the containment trench has not been inspected below grade; this hold blocks clearance until resolved [GARNET]. Staleness applies: any candidate-bound hydraulic, pump, treatment, or source-water change invalidates prior sampling rounds and requires re-sampling per CIPHER protocol [CIPHER, MOSAIC].\",\"old\":\"Clearance requires two complete sampling rounds spaced at least sixteen hours apart; one clean round is insufficient [CIPHER]. The benzene reporting limit is 0.5 micrograms per liter and the action threshold is 5.0 micrograms per liter; these are distinct controls and must not be conflated, as a reporting limit indicates analytical capability while an action threshold triggers public-health response [CIPHER]. CIPHER sample placement consumes BASTION flow paths and GARNET contamination hypotheses; any change to hydraulic routing or source-water characterization makes prior sampling evidence stale [CIPHER, BASTION, GARNET]. Current volatile-organic screening is non-detect at the reservoir and 3.1 micrograms per liter at the river intake; these locations must not be merged, as they represent distinct hydrological positions with different contamination probabilities [GARNET]. The ash-plume model assigns a 34 percent chance of intake impact during the next rain event, which is a forecast probability, not measured contamination [GARNET]. The investigation hold remains active for the chemical yard because the containment trench has not been inspected below grade; this hold blocks clearance until resolved [GARNET]. Staleness applies: any candidate-bound hydraulic, pump, treatment, or source-water change invalidates prior sampling rounds and requires re-sampling per CIPHER protocol [CIPHER, MOSAIC].\"},{\"new\":\"Emergency generation can carry 4.1 MW for thirty-six hours at the current fuel stock; the duration is not thirty-six days and excludes mobile-pump load [ECHO]. The grid feed is rated at 8.2 MW, but the damaged switchgear limits current delivery to 6.5 MW; rated power is not usable power [ECHO]. KESTREL diesel stock covers emergency load for 36 hours and full pump load for 22 hours; these load regimes are distinct and must not be merged as a single duration [KESTREL, ECHO]. Coagulant and chlorine chemical stocks provide 4.5 days and 3.2 days of treatment capacity respectively; inventory duration is not delivery lead time [KESTREL, FALCON]. Staffing continuity depends on mutual-aid logistics; current shift coverage must be verified against the 36-hour generator window [KESTREL]. Any change to ECHO power state or KESTREL fuel stock makes prior pump sequencing evidence stale [DELTA, ECHO, KESTREL]. The shared-main limit under staged demand requires that DELTA pump capacity not exceed the 14.5 MGD transmission main cap; the latest inspected configuration sustained 12.8 MGD after fire-flow and hospital reserve [DELTA, ECHO, BASTION]. Restoration may advance through 10, 30, 60, and 100 percent demand stages only after two consecutive 30-minute windows meet pressure, turbidity, power, and storage gates [DELTA, BASTION, FALCON, ECHO]. Pump nameplate capacities cannot be summed into usable system flow; the shared-main limit governs actual deliverable capacity [DELTA].\",\"old\":\"Emergency generation can carry 4.1 MW for thirty-six hours at the current fuel stock; the duration is not thirty-six days and excludes mobile-pump load [ECHO]. The grid feed is rated at 8.2 MW, but the damaged switchgear limits current delivery to 6.5 MW; rated power is not usable power [ECHO]. KESTREL diesel stock covers emergency load for 36 hours and full pump load for 22 hours; these load regimes are distinct and must not be merged as a single duration [KESTREL, ECHO]. Coagulant and chlorine chemical stocks provide 4.5 days and 3.2 days of treatment capacity respectively; inventory duration is not delivery lead time [KESTREL, FALCON]. Staffing continuity depends on mutual-aid logistics; current shift coverage must be verified against the 36-hour generator window [KESTREL]. Any change to ECHO power state or KESTREL fuel stock makes prior pump sequencing evidence stale [DELTA, ECHO, KESTREL]. The shared-main limit under staged demand requires that DELTA pump capacity not exceed the 6.5 MW usable grid feed plus any available generator margin without triggering cavitation constraints [DELTA, ECHO, BASTION].\"},{\"new\":\"Critical-signal telemetry coverage stands at 96 percent, with 4 percent of monitored points unobserved; this coverage gap does not prove the uninstrumented zones are healthy, only that they are not currently observable [INDIGO]. Pressure warnings at 38 psi and isolation triggers at 30 psi are distinct control states; a warning does not initiate isolation, and an isolation trigger does not merely warn [INDIGO, BASTION]. Telemetry delay is 700 milliseconds at p95 and 1,200 milliseconds at p99; both are observations, not control deadlines, and must be accounted for when evaluating alert-to-action timing [INDIGO]. Warnings versus actions: a pressure warning at 38 psi requires monitoring and logging but does not require isolation; an isolation trigger at 30 psi requires immediate valve closure and zone separation [INDIGO, BASTION]. Any change to hydraulic model H12, pump plan P9, or control-system key set W4 invalidates prior telemetry correlation evidence and requires re-verification of coverage and alert thresholds [MOSAIC, INDIGO, BASTION, DELTA, ECHO, FALCON]. Alternate telemetry paths must be identified for the 4 percent unobserved zone before closure can be claimed [INDIGO, NEXUS].\",\"old\":\"Critical-signal telemetry coverage stands at 96 percent, with 4 percent of monitored points unobserved; this coverage gap does not prove the uninstrumented zones are healthy, only that they are not currently observable [INDIGO]. Pressure warnings at 38 psi and isolation triggers at 30 psi are distinct control states; a warning does not initiate isolation, and an isolation trigger does not merely warn [INDIGO, BASTION]. Telemetry delays in the unobserved 4 percent create uncertainty that must be treated as potential adverse condition until verified by direct observation or alternate telemetry [INDIGO]. Warnings versus actions: a pressure warning at 38 psi requires monitoring and logging but does not require isolation; an isolation trigger at 30 psi requires immediate valve closure and zone separation [INDIGO, BASTION]. Any change to hydraulic model H12, pump plan P9, or control-system key set W4 invalidates prior telemetry correlation evidence and requires re-verification of coverage and alert thresholds [MOSAIC, INDIGO, BASTION, DELTA, ECHO, FALCON]. Alternate telemetry paths must be identified for the 4 percent unobserved zone before closure can be claimed [INDIGO, NEXUS].\"}]}"}
